  "content": "To find the perimeter of the regular hexagon, we will follow these steps:\n\n1.  **Determine the side length of the equilateral triangle.**\n    The problem states that the regular hexagon is divided into six equilateral triangles. An equilateral triangle has three sides of equal length. Let $s$ be the side length of one of these triangles.\n    \n    The perimeter of one equilateral triangle is given as 21 inches. Therefore:\n    $$3s = 21$$\n    \n    Solving for $s$:\n    $$s = \\frac{21}{3} = 7 \\text{ inches}$$\n\n2.  **Determine the side length of the regular hexagon.**\n    A regular hexagon can be divided into six equilateral triangles by drawing lines from the center to each vertex. The sides of these triangles that form the boundary of the hexagon are the sides of the hexagon itself. Therefore, the side length of the regular hexagon is equal to the side length of the equilateral triangle.\n    \n    So, the side length of the hexagon is also $7$ inches.\n\n3.  **Calculate the perimeter of the regular hexagon.**\n    A regular hexagon has 6 equal sides. Let $P_{hex}$ be the perimeter of the hexagon.\n    $$P_{hex} = 6 \\times s$$\n    $$P_{hex} = 6 \\times 7$$\n    $$P_{hex} = 42 \\text{ inches}$$\n\n**Conclusion:**\nThe perimeter of the regular hexagon is 42 inches.\n\n\\boxed{42}",
